 **`@hangar adr` drafts a decision record from a Slack thread** (#170). In a thread on a mapped channel, `@hangar adr [title]` runs a job that adds one `docs/decisions/NNNN-<slug>.md` in the [ADR format](docs/templates/adr.md). The thread reaches the agent only as the untrusted, redacted `slack-thread.md`, the agent has egress `none`, and hangar opens the PR after the turn: a draft (the job is tainted) titled `ADR: <title>`, labelled `agent-drafted` and `adr`, linking the thread. Hangar checks the file as the job's verify (one new file, the next number, frontmatter and sections, and valid YAML in an optional `checks:` block, which flightdeck runs). A change outside `docs/decisions/` opens as a draft with a packet warning. An agent-drafted PR never lands on its requester's approval, whatever `self_approval` says. For any PR, removing or changing a line of an existing ADR's `checks:` block makes the packet's risk `high`. A repo without `docs/decisions/` is refused ([slack.md](docs/slack.md#hangar-adr-a-thread-becomes-a-decision-record), [landing.md](docs/landing.md#agent-drafted-adrs)).
 
-**Fixes:** `hangar doctor` no longer fails a fresh laptop install on the intake posture: without the git gateway it warns until an intake front door is configured, then fails naming those doors, with one fix command (#236, PR #247). `hangar harness freeze --out DIR` checks a directory that already holds a lock against it first, and refuses to overwrite uncommitted edits there unless `--force` (#251). `deploy.yml` deploys again: GitHub dropped the control-plane job's image output because it contained the `HANGAR_REGISTRY` secret, so every approved rollout set an empty image and rolled back; only the digest now crosses jobs and the deploy job rebuilds the reference, refusing an empty or partial one before touching the cluster. A server too old to report its power state no longer makes a merge's deploy skip when the state job read the cluster's labels as up. On GKE, exec streams use their own Kubernetes API client: two execs connecting at once could leave the shared client on the websocket transport for good, after which every pod list and read failed with `Handshake status 200 OK`, stopping the reconciler, hangar-envd and sandbox readiness (found by the first live acceptance run). The acceptance backup check accepts the server's `GCS bucket gs://…` wording. Open-egress sandboxes on GKE get hangar's git gateway (and an in-cluster OTel collector) in `/etc/hosts`: Agent Sandbox's managed policy resolves internet names only, so with the gateway on every clone failed with `Could not resolve host: hangar-gateway.hangar.svc`. An environment saved through the API that extends a shipped one finds the skills it inherits: their paths are relative to the parent's file, not the child's on the data volume (`skill path not found: skills/repo-conventions`).
+**Fixes:** `hangar doctor` no longer fails a fresh laptop install on the intake posture: without the git gateway it warns until an intake front door is configured, then fails naming those doors, with one fix command (#236, PR #247). `hangar harness freeze --out DIR` checks a directory that already holds a lock against it first, and refuses to overwrite uncommitted edits there unless `--force` (#251). `deploy.yml` deploys again: GitHub dropped the control-plane job's image output because it contained the `HANGAR_REGISTRY` secret, so every approved rollout set an empty image and rolled back; only the digest now crosses jobs and the deploy job rebuilds the reference, refusing an empty or partial one before touching the cluster. A server too old to report its power state no longer makes a merge's deploy skip when the state job read the cluster's labels as up. On GKE, exec streams use their own Kubernetes API client: two execs connecting at once could leave the shared client on the websocket transport for good, after which every pod list and read failed with `Handshake status 200 OK`, stopping the reconciler, hangar-envd and sandbox readiness (found by the first live acceptance run). The acceptance backup check accepts the server's `GCS bucket gs://…` wording. Open-egress sandboxes on GKE get hangar's git gateway (and an in-cluster OTel collector) in `/etc/hosts`: Agent Sandbox's managed policy resolves internet names only, so with the gateway on every clone failed with `Could not resolve host: hangar-gateway.hangar.svc`. An environment saved through the API that extends a shipped one finds the skills it inherits: their paths are relative to the parent's file, not the child's on the data volume (`skill path not found: skills/repo-conventions`). A harness registry in a subdirectory of a repo (`HANGAR_HARNESS_REPO` set to a directory inside a checkout) resolves older revisions again: `name@rN`, `name@sha256:` and channels on a lock that isn't the current one read the registry's history relative to the registry, not the repo's root (#228).
 
